          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioners (Solicitor General Paul D. Clement), according to Petitioners (Solicitor General Paul D. Clement), general Clement explained that the Supreme Court\u2019s decision in Flast v. Cohen recognized a narrow circumstance where a taxpayer may have standing to challenge congressional spending, rejecting broader suggestions for taxpayer standing in Establishment Clause cases."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "a86e03f7916da729be4a29a7597f3e75709d5a0681ace114d5ef000ed00a600a",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-a86e03f7916d-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 4,
              "start": 4
            }
          },
          {
            "document_hash": "a86e03f7916da729be4a29a7597f3e75709d5a0681ace114d5ef000ed00a600a",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-a86e03f7916d-0000-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 6,
              "start": 6
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justices asked whether a congressional program that favors religion is unconstitutional, but a presidential implementation that favors religion might be permissible, raising the question of who may have standing to sue."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "a86e03f7916da729be4a29a7597f3e75709d5a0681ace114d5ef000ed00a600a",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-a86e03f7916d-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 27,
              "start": 27
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Scalia, justice Scalia further questioned whether a taxpayer\u2019s upset over government spending could itself provide Article\u202fIII standing."
      }
    ],
